    "knowledge_point": "Computing the Probability of a Geometric Random Variable at a Point",
    "problem": "At an ice cream shop, on average, $25%$ of customers buy chocolate ice cream. What is the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first person buying a chocolate ice cream? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.",
    "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers that enter the ice cream shop until the first one buys a chocolate ice cream. Since the chance of a customer buying a chocolate ice cream is $25%,$ the number of customers before the first buys a chocolate ice cream can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=25%=\\frac{25}{100}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.25)^{x-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first buying a chocolate ice cream is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{4-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{3}(0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.105\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
